PRDs as human-owned by design, not technical limitation, and the planning constitution allows documentation tasks. I'll propose it and let the project owner decide if they want to reject it.\n\nLooking at what a stitch agent can actually do—reading required_reading files, creating or modifying listed files, following requirements and design decisions, meeting acceptance criteria—a PRD task is perfectly feasible. The agent would read existing PRDs to understand the format, review the architecture docs, create the new file, and follow the PRD format rules. Since `true` is straightforward, no source code analysis is needed.\n\nI should reconsider the ordering though. The use cases are already sequenced with `true` first, and the canonical ordering rule says documentation comes before code, so the PRD task should be the initial one.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the actual requirements for the `true` utility: it needs to return exit status 0, handle the standard GNU flags like --help and --version, accept and ignore any operands, and properly handle error cases. The implementation is essentially just a main function that checks for those flags and returns success.\n\nFor the PRD task, I need to make sure the stitch agent has access to existing PRD examples so it understands the project's documentation format, along with the architecture and vision docs for context. The PRD itself should cover the problem statement, goals around matching GNU behavior exactly, and the specific requirements for exit codes and flag handling.\n\nI'm second-guessing whether documentation is the right priority though. The pipeline indicates specs should come before implementation, and rel02.0 is pending specs, so a PRD does fit the sequence. The architecture notes that PRDs are human-authored, but the planning constitution warns against proposing tasks requiring human judgment—though defining a trivial utility like `true` doesn't really demand that kind of judgment. I think I should go ahead with proposing the PRD.\n\nNow I'm looking more carefully at the discrepancy between the codestatus showing \"not started\" and the actual source code that's provided. The implementation_status clarifies that generated code exists on the generation-merged tags but not on the current working branch, which is a generation branch itself. So the source code I'm seeing might be from a different branch state than what's currently active.\n\nBut given that we're on the generation branch and git status shows clean, I should trust that the source code provided represents the actual current state. So I'll treat rel00.0 through rel01.3 as complete.\n\nFor my next task, I'm proposing to write the PRD for the `true` utility, which is the first documentation deliverable needed to unblock rel02.0. I'll structure it following the existing PRD format, referencing the cat PRD as a template and pulling from the architecture and vision docs, plus the existing cat implementation as a pattern reference.\n\nI'm planning to create the PRD file with all required sections—Problem, Goals, Requirements, Non-Goals, and Acceptance Criteria—covering the true utility's behaviors from GNU coreutils, including exit status handling, flag parsing for --help and --version, and operand ignoring, with acceptance criteria tied to differential testing against gtrue.\n\nBut I'm realizing I need to think about what existing PRD files the stitch agent should reference—the source code only included Go files, not the actual PRD contents from docs/specs/product-requirements/, so I'm not certain what those files contain exactly.\n\nThe stitch agent will need to read at least one existing PRD to understand the format, and since the constraint says it can't rely on context from this conversation, the task description needs to be completely self-contained. Looking at the existing PRD numbers, there are gaps in the sequence, so I'll need to pick a logical next number rather than assuming strict sequentiality.\n\nI should verify the naming convention matches the documented format of prd[NNN]-<utility>.yaml, and I'm realizing I need to check if there's a specific PRD format file the stitch agent should follow when generating the new one. The true utility itself is straightforward to define since it just exits with 0 in most cases, so the stitch agent should be able to produce a correct PRD for it.\n\nFor the design decisions, I should stick to what's derived from the architecture and existing patterns rather than inventing new ones—so I'll reference the YAML format used in existing PRDs like prd006-cat.yaml as the template, and ensure differential testing acceptance criteria align with what's specified in ARCHITECTURE.yaml.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether a PRD for `true` is even necessary given how trivial the command is, since the project methodology typically derives PRDs from C source analysis and I'd be writing one from scratch. But the process requires PRDs first, so I'll need to make the task description clear enough that the stitch agent can produce a properly formatted PRD that maps to the project's standards, including the well-known behavior of GNU true: exit 0, with --help and --version only processed as sole arguments. I'm laying out the required reading materials that establish the PRD format, architectural constraints around differential testing, and the existing implementation patterns I need to follow. The PRD for `true` needs to cover all GNU behaviors with sections addressing the core problem and goals around matching exit codes and test coverage.\n\nNow I'm defining the specific requirements: the `true` utility must unconditionally exit with status 0, handle `--help` and `--version` flags by printing to stdout and exiting cleanly, silently ignore all other arguments, and properly handle SIGPIPE signals like the other utilities in the codebase. the project, per pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler pattern), stderr error format (matching \"true: <message>\" pattern from other utilities), and exit code contract (always 0 for true). I should include acceptance criteria verifiable through differential testing against the GNU reference binary gtrue from Homebrew coreutils, running all tests with LC_ALL=C per ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD6. I'm following the YAML PRD format from prd006-cat.yaml with the same section ordering and requirement numbering scheme, and since true is one of the five trivial utilities in rel02.0, the PRD should stay concise given its minimal flag interaction and lack of complex edge cases. Now I'm laying out the acceptance criteria to verify the PRD file exists at the correct path, contains all required sections, has sequentially numbered requirements specific enough to drive code generation, and meets the other validation checks. I'm verifying the YAML structure has the required `id` and `text` fields for all mappings, using literal block scalars for multi-line content. For the PRD number, prd020 seems like the safest choice since prd019 exists as an orphaned entry and I'm not certain about the availability of numbers 012-018. I need to verify I have the authority to assign a PRD number following the naming convention prd[NNN]-[utility-name], and I'm confident this is a documentation task rather than a code implementation task since the PRD doesn't exist yet and the analysis shows all rel02.0 use cases are pending. I should also check that I'm not violating the constraint about external infrastructure components before finalizing the output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":105684,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f60af45-df2d-4c4f-b2ba-e8d79c88e89c","uuid":"711daf36-9753-43bd-85e1-5b3365707b99"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Kgnj3oWsP8msxaXho5KXyK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs accessible to implementers. This project derives specifications from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds per-utility Go implementations with differential tests. `pkg/testutils/` provides the differential testing harness. `pkg/sys/` wraps platform-specific syscalls. `pkg/format/` provides shared output formatting. `docs/` holds human-authored specs (PRDs, use cases, test suites). `magefiles/` is external scaffold infrastructure.\n\n**Current State (trusting source_code):** All code for rel00.0 through rel01.3 exists — `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/version`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du` are all implemented with tests.\n\n**Next Release:** rel02.0 (Batch 2 — trivial utilities: true, false, yes, basename, dirname). All five use cases have `specstatus: pending` — no PRDs exist yet. Per the pipeline (P5: PRDs before code), the first step is writing PRDs.\n\n**Priority Reasoning:** The earliest incomplete release is rel02.0. The pipeline requires specs before code. The first use case is `rel02.0-uc001-true`. GNU `true` is the simplest possible utility (exit 0, handle --help/--version as sole argument, ignore everything else), making its PRD a safe documentation task with zero ambiguity.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"prd020-true: Write PRD for true utility (rel02.0-uc001-true)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: documentation\n    format_rule: prd-format\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd006-cat.yaml (PRD format reference -- follow the same YAML structure, section ordering, and requirement numbering scheme)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (design decisions DD2 differential testing, DD4 Homebrew GNU reference binaries, DD5 human-guided specification, DD6 LC_ALL=C constraint)\n      - docs/VISION.yaml (success criteria SC1 differential test parity, SC2 PRD coverage)\n      - cmd/cat/main.go (existing utility implementation pattern -- SIGPIPE handler via pkg/sys, manual parseArgs, progName constant, exit code contract)\n\n    files:\n      - path: docs/specs/product-requirements/prd020-true.yaml\n        action: create\n        note: PRD for the true utility covering all GNU true behaviors\n\n    required_sections:\n      - \"Problem: true must match GNU true behavior exactly for differential testing\"\n      - \"Goals: G1 match GNU true exit behavior, G2 match GNU true flag handling\"\n      - \"Requirements: R1.x covering exit status, flag handling, operand behavior, SIGPIPE, error format\"\n      - \"Non-Goals: what true does not implement\"\n      - \"Acceptance Criteria: checkable outcomes including differential test parity\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Write a PRD covering GNU true core behavior: true exits with status 0\n          unconditionally. When invoked with exactly one argument that is \"--help\",\n          it prints a usage message to stdout and exits 0. When invoked with exactly\n          one argument that is \"--version\", it prints version information to stdout\n          and exits 0. All other arguments (including unrecognized flags) are\n          silently ignored and true still exits 0.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Include requirements for SIGPIPE handling (silent exit matching other\n          utilities in the project, per pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler pattern),\n          and the exit code contract (always 0 regardless of arguments or input).\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Include acceptance criteria that are verifiable through differential\n          testing against the GNU reference binary gtrue (Homebrew coreutils).\n          All differential tests must run with LC_ALL=C per ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD6.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Follow the YAML PRD format established by prd006-cat.yaml. Use the same\n          section ordering, requirement numbering scheme (R1, R1.1, R1.2, etc.),\n          and acceptance criteria format.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          The GNU reference binary is gtrue (Homebrew coreutils, g-prefixed),\n          per ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD4.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          true is one of the five trivial utilities in rel02.0. The PRD should be\n          concise -- true has minimal flag interaction and no complex edge cases.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: PRD file exists at docs/specs/product-requirements/prd020-true.yaml and is valid YAML\n      - id: AC2\n        text: PRD contains all required sections -- Problem, Goals, Requirements, Non-Goals, Acceptance Criteria\n      - id: AC3\n        text: Requirements are numbered sequentially (R1, R1.1, etc.) and each is specific enough to drive code generation without ambiguity\n      - id: AC4\n        text: Acceptance criteria reference differential testing against gtrue with LC_ALL=C\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":105684,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f60af45-df2d-4c4f-b2ba-e8d79c88e89c","uuid":"81e64a41-86d1-44a7-89b7-1c87a6ffd089"}
